My research sits at the intersection of violence, safeguarding, and public health. It focuses on how violence and vulnerability are measured, understood, and addressed across academic, policy, and practice contexts.

## Childhood exposure to domestic violence and abuse

My doctoral research examined childhood exposure to domestic violence and abuse in low- and middle-income countries, using Violence Against Children Surveys data to explore prevalence, risk factors, and mental health outcomes.

## Stalking and coercive control

I contribute to research and evaluation on stalking, coercive control, and multi-agency responses to harm, including work linked to intervention evaluation and outcome measurement.

## Violence prevention and safeguarding systems

My work examines how services, institutions, and multi-agency systems respond to violence and vulnerability, with a focus on producing evidence that can inform policy and practice.

## Global health and violence research

I am also interested in global health approaches to violence prevention, including work on disability, structural inequalities, and climate-related risks.
